# Tessera stdlib: SignalCaster
#
# A condition variable for an ExclusiveLock, in one U32: a sequence number that every
# wake changes. A waiter reads it while it holds the mutex, unlocks, and sleeps
# (wait_while: a thread sleeps, a fiber parks) while it still holds what it read, so a wake that comes between the
# unlock and the sleep has changed it and the sleep returns at once: no wake
# is lost. A wait may also return without a wake, so a waiter checks its
# condition again in a loop. The same design as Rust's futex condition
# variable, the same on every operating system. It starts with seq 0 and needs
# nothing to free.

module Standard/Os

### A condition variable: threads wait on it with a {ExclusiveLock} held until another thread changes what they wait for and
### wakes them. A waiter holds the mutex, checks its condition, and calls {SignalCaster.wait} while it doesn't hold,
### again after every return: a wait may return without a wake, and another thread may have taken what it was woken
### for. A waker changes the condition with the mutex held, then calls {SignalCaster.cast_one} or {SignalCaster.cast_all},
### with the mutex held or after releasing it. No wake is lost: a waiter that checked before the change is woken.
### One U32 that every wake changes. It starts at 0, needs nothing to free, and works the same on every operating
### system.
record SignalCaster
    seq: U32

### A condition with no waiters.
### :returns: A {SignalCaster} with seq 0.
common routine SignalCaster.construct() -> Me
    c: Me = Me(seq: 0)
    return c

### Releases `mutex`, sleeps until a wake, and takes `mutex` again before returning. It may return without a wake,
### so the caller checks its condition again in a loop.
### :param mutex: The mutex the caller holds, which guards the condition.
routine @SignalCaster.wait(mutex: @ExclusiveLock) -> None
    seen: U32 = me.seq.peek_atomic()
    mutex.unlock()
    wait_while(at: me.seq, expected: seen)
    mutex.lock()
    return

### As {SignalCaster.wait}, but gives up `timeout` after the call (it may wait a little longer, down to
### the operating system's clock unit, never forever). It takes `mutex` again before returning either way.
### :param mutex: The mutex the caller holds, which guards the condition.
### :param timeout: The longest wait.
### :returns: Whether it returned before the timeout passed: false means the time is up, true means woken (or
### returned without a wake) in time, and the caller checks its condition again.
routine @SignalCaster.wait_for(mutex: @ExclusiveLock, timeout: Duration) -> Bool
    deadline: Duration = Clock.monotonic() +^ timeout
    return me.wait_until(mutex, deadline)

### As {SignalCaster.wait}, but gives up at `deadline` on the {Clock.monotonic} clock. A loop that waits for a condition
### keeps one deadline across its waits, so the wakes that don't satisfy it don't extend the wait. It takes `mutex`
### again before returning either way, and releases it even when the deadline has already passed.
### :param mutex: The mutex the caller holds, which guards the condition.
### :param deadline: When to give up, in {Clock.monotonic} time.
### :returns: Whether it returned before the deadline: false means the time is up, true means woken (or returned
### without a wake) in time, and the caller checks its condition again.
routine @SignalCaster.wait_until(mutex: @ExclusiveLock, deadline: Duration) -> Bool
    seen: U32 = me.seq.peek_atomic()
    mutex.unlock()
    now: Duration = Clock.monotonic()
    left: Duration = deadline -^ now
    wait_while_for(at: me.seq, expected: seen, timeout: left)
    mutex.lock()
    return Clock.monotonic() < deadline

### Wakes one thread waiting on the condition, if any. Call it after changing the condition with the mutex held.
routine @SignalCaster.cast_one() -> None
    me.seq.atomic_fetch_add(1)
    wake_one(me.seq)
    return

### Wakes every thread waiting on the condition. Call it after changing the condition with the mutex held.
routine @SignalCaster.cast_all() -> None
    me.seq.atomic_fetch_add(1)
    wake_all(me.seq)
    return
